Description:
The function table_column in http://moodle.cvs.sourceforge.net/moodle/moodle/lib/datalib.php is a liar :)



Basically, if the updating of the table column has failed, this function should return false.



But, for example, if it _does_ fail for postgres, the return value is:



    return execute_sql('COMMIT');



This is not correct. If you issue a commit against postgres, *even if the transaction has failed* the *COMMIT* in itself will be a success. Admittely it will perform a ROLLBACK instead of a COMMIT, but nevertheless, the return value of table_column will pretty much always be true regardless of whether the column was actually updated :)



I'm not sure how this affects mysql or other databases, somebody should probably test it. The way I triggered it was by doing:



table_column('table', 'oldfield', 'newname');



Where 'oldfield' was a text field. The call above will try to make the field into an INT field...



The CORRECT way to fix this is probably to use ADODB's SmartTrans for transactions. For example:



$db->StartTrans();

$db->Execute(...);

$db->Execute(...);

$failed = $db->HasFailedTrans();

$db->CompleteTrans();



If the transaction succeeds, $failed will be empty and the transaction will be completed. If it fails, $failed will be true and CompleteTrans will perform a ROLLBACK.



This is the most database-independent way to fix this.



Sorry I can't prepare a patch right now, but pretty much what you do is replace:



    execute_sql('BEGIN');



with



    $db->StartTrans();



and



    return execute_sql('COMMIT');



with



            if ($db->HasFailedTrans()) {

                $db->CompleteTrans();

                return false;

            }

            $db->CompleteTrans();

            return true;



Or similar.



As mentioned, not tested for mysql.

