Description:
Couse professors have access to a "Files" interface which allows them access to a hierarchy of files and folders that actually live on disk on the web server. They can create folders and files there, and this is nice. 

However, Moodle itself also creates folders in this hierarchy, but when it does, it uses numeric id's to name the folders representing courses or assignments. Since professors have no reason to know what these numeric id's represent, it would be good to give the administrator the option to tell moodle "create friendly directory names". This opens doors for modules like uploadsingle, and my module (uploadmulti, in contrib) to take advantage of the filesystem and still provide a usable interface to our assignment types' uploads. I

I've implemented this, and it's been in production since 1.6.1. It works well, and requires a very minimal amount of code. I don't know if this is better implemented in the config.php file or the web-based admin interface. My implementation uses config.php. Here's what I did: 

1. I added a config.php line that looks like this: 
$CFG->friendlydirnames true

2. I altered /mod/assignment/lib.php's file_area_name function so it looks like this: 

function file_area_name($userid) {
        global $CFG;

        if($CFG->friendlydirnames == 'true'){
            if(is_numeric($userid)){
                $uname = get_field('user','username','id',$userid);
            }else{
                $uname = $userid; 
            }
            return $this->course->id.'/'.$CFG->moddata.'/assignment/'.$this->assignment->name.'/'.$uname; 
        }else{
            return $this->course->id.'/'.$CFG->moddata.'/assignment/'.$this->assignment->id.'/'.$userid;
        }
    }

This allows for the 'false' option, in which case moodle acts just like it does today by default. Further, just this small change insures that the rest of moodle can find or create directories, because any time a directory name is being searched for *or* created, file_area_name is responsible for delivering the name of the directory. 

This is useful to me because my profs all use uploads, and if they can't figure out how to find their files in the web-based interface, they come looking for a filesystem mount or login access to get to the files on the server, which is bad, because file management in this case should be restricted to moodle only. 

Thanks for your consideration. 
brian.

I would like to suggest that the new code to amend and log dates that recently has been added to the "restorelib.php" scripts for all Moodle modules be moved to a separate function.

This would have several advantages:
    - you can check the validity of the date fields. For example, it makes no sense to increment the date field if it is empty
    - improve the readability of the "restorelib.php" scripts, which have become much harder to read-at-a-glance due to the plethora of recenlty inserted "fwrite" statements. 
    - allow the message to be printed in the admin's own language 
    - the Moodle core programmers can control and therefore standardize how the $restorelog is accessed and how the messages in it are formatted

I would like to propose a new function for the restore library. It needs to be called only once for each record, before the record is restored. When passed a list of datefields and the XML tree, it would modify the date fields in the XML tree and write to the restore log. 

For example to modify the dates in Assignment records:
    backup_dates('Assignment', $restore, $info['MOD']['#'], array('TIMEDUE', 'TIMEAVAILABLE'));

Or for Hotpot records:
    backup_dates(
        'Hotpot', $restore, $xml, array('TIMEOPEN', 'TIMECLOSE', 'TIMECREATED', 'TIMEMODIFIED')
    );

Or for workshop records:
    backup_dates(
        'Workshop', $restore, $info['MOD']['#'], 
        array('SUBMISSIONSTART', 'ASSESSMENTSTART', 'SUBMISSIONEND', 'ASSESSMENTEND', 'RELEASEGRADES')
    );

Or for workshop submissions records :
    backup_dates('Workshop Submission', $restore, $sub_info['#'], array('TIMECREATED'));

