12/Oct/06 6:34 AM;712020:c6549e6d-0268-4fa0-92ef-8a6ba41c4c54;> It is notable that the structure of the Hotpot module is markedly different from the rest, 
> this is a matter of some discussion here, we wonder is there a particular reason for that. 

Probably the reason is that when I was writing the backuplib.php and restorelib.php scripts for the HotPot module, I wanted to use central functions, "hotpot_backup_records" and "hotpot_restore_records", to do the repetitive work of backing up and restoring the fields in the records.

I looked at the other modules, but they seemed to contain many hardcoded fwrite statements (in the backuplib.php) and hard-coded assignment statements in the restorelib.php. It struck me that if fields were added renamed or deleted, this would require laborious and error prone fixing of these hard-coded strings.

Basically, I wanted to be able to say just backup (or restore) all the fields in such-and-such a table, and leave the central functions to work out the field names. 

Once these central functions are in place, a table can be backed up with a single function call. For example the hotpot_attempts table is backed up and restored as follows:

    function hotpot_backup_attempts($bf, &$parent, $level, $status) {
        // $parent is a reference to a hotpot record

        $more_backup = ''
        .    'hotpot_backup_details($bf, $record, $level, $status);'
        .    'hotpot_backup_responses($bf, $record, $level, $status);'
        ;

        $status = hotpot_backup_records(
            $bf, $status, $level, 'hotpot_attempts', "hotpot=$parent->id", 
            'ATTEMPT_DATA', array(), 'ATTEMPT', array(), array('hotpot'), $more_backup
        );
        return $status;
    }


    function hotpot_restore_attempts(&$restore, $status, &$xml, &$record, $hotpot_v20=false) {
        // $xml is an XML tree for a hotpot record
        // $record is the newly added hotpot record

        $foreignkeys = array(
            'userid'=>'user',
            'hotpot'=>$record->id,
        );

        $more_restore = 'hotpot_restore_details($restore, $status, $xml, $record);';

        $status = hotpot_restore_records(
            $restore, $status, $xml, 'hotpot_attempts', $foreignkeys, $more_restore, 'ATTEMPT_DATA', 'ATTEMPT'
        );
        return $status;
    }

Basically the "hotpot_backup_records" and " hotpot_restore_records" functions make the backup and restore more modular and so more robust and easier to maintain centrally. If something like these functions were available with the "backup/lib.php" then all modules could use them, without having to worry about a single fwrite and the only field names to worry about would be the keys.

