Description:
I have written the function user_update($olduser, $newuser) inside the moodle/auth/db/auth.php in order to make the external db authentication system able to modify user records on the external db. In this way, when a user changes something inside his profile these changes are saved also inside the external db used to authenticate the user.
I have made this change in moodle 1.8 and added the function user_update() as a method of the class that identifies the authentication system. This is the function (I used the same function from the ldap system as a starting point):

     /* 
     * user_update saves userinformation from moodle to external db
     *
     * Called when the user record is updated.
     * Modifies user in external database. It takes olduser (before changes) and newuser (after changes) 
     * conpares information saved modified information to external db.
     *
     * @param mixed $olduser     Userobject before modifications
     * @param mixed $newuser     Userobject new modified userobject
     * @return boolean result
     *
     */
    function user_update($olduser, $newuser) {

        global $USER, $CFG;

        // Connect to the external database (forcing new connection)
        $authdb = &ADONewConnection($this->config->type);
        $authdb->Connect($this->config->host, $this->config->user, $this->config->pass, $this->config->name, true); 
        $authdb->SetFetchMode(ADODB_FETCH_ASSOC);

        $fields = array("firstname", "lastname", "email", "phone1", "phone2", "department", "address", "city", "country", "description", "idnumber", "lang"); 

        // Create the array that will map the fields contained inside the $fields array
        // with the fields inside the external db
	$moodleattributes = array();
        foreach ($fields as $field) {
            if (!empty($this->config->{"field_map_$field"})) {
                $moodleattributes[$field] = $this->config->{"field_map_$field"};
                if (preg_match('/,/',$moodleattributes[$field])) {
                    $moodleattributes[$field] = explode(',', $moodleattributes[$field]); // split ?
                }
            }
        }

                // Initialize some variables used to create the query
		$update = '';
		$ischange = false;

		// Create the query for the update 
		foreach ($moodleattributes as $key => $dbkeys) {

            // only process if the moodle field ($key) has changed and we
            // are set to update external db with it
            if ($olduser->$key !== $newuser->$key and
                !empty($this->config->{'field_updateremote_'. $key})) {
				if($ischange){
					$update .= ", ".$dbkeys;
					$update .= " = '".$newuser->$key."'";
				} else {
					$update .= $dbkeys." = '".$newuser->$key."'";
					$ischange = true;
				}
			}
        }

		$updateqry = "UPDATE {$this->config->table} SET ".$update." WHERE {$this->config->fielduser} = '".$olduser->username."'";

		if(!$rs = $authdb->Execute($updateqry))
			error ('Error in executing the update on the external db -> '.$updateqry);

		$authdb->Close();		
        return true;
    }

Of course, in order to choose which fields can be changed and saved also on the external db, instead only inside the internal, I have modified the page moodle/auth/db/config.html around line 196:

print_auth_lock_options('db', $user_fields, get_string('auth_dbextrafields', 'auth'), true, true);

In this way when we configure the db auth system we can choose if we want to save changes on the external db just like the ldap auth system configuration.

