When creating a module settings page using the adminlib functionality, it is possible to validate fields for example as an INT, as TEXT, as BOOL using a parameter such as PARAM_BOOL as defined in moodlelib.php - an example of this would be

      $settings->add(new admin_setting_configtext('facetoface_fromaddress', get_string('setting:fromaddress_caption', 'facetoface'),get_string('setting:fromaddress', 'facetoface'), get_string('setting:fromaddressdefault', 'facetoface'), PARAM_TEXT));

Now it is also possible to validate the text using a regular expression, as follows:

      $settings->add(new admin_setting_configtext('facetoface_fromaddress', get_string('setting:fromaddress_caption', 'facetoface'),get_string('setting:fromaddress', 'facetoface'), get_string('setting:fromaddressdefault', 'facetoface'), "/^((?:[\w\.\-])+\@(?:(?:[a-zA-Z\d\-])+\.)+(?:[a-zA-Z\d]{2,4}))$/"));

This could further be refactored as follows:

      $settings->add(new admin_setting_configtext('facetoface_fromaddress', get_string('setting:fromaddress_caption', 'facetoface'),get_string('setting:fromaddress', 'facetoface'), get_string('setting:fromaddressdefault', 'facetoface'), PARAM_EMAIL));

with PARAM_EMAIL a constant in moodlelib.php, containing the regular expression.  I believe this makes the form code more readable as it is immediately apparent what the check is doing.

