we mostly use Hebrew in our Moodle site but some of the courses are for learning the English language
so we use mixed Hebrew and English activity and resource's names that show up at the navigation bar
in a confusing order, although in the right and correct order according to the bidi standard as defined by
the W3C and implemented CORRECTLY by Moodle developers.
(bidi standards : http://www.w3.org/TR/i18n-html-tech-bidi/ )

BUT ! it is more visibility useful to display the navigation links in a mixed case scenario without considering those
standards and definitions. for example, a correct bidi navigation sentence starting with 2 hebrew words (rtl) and then 3
English words (ltr) would be displayed like this:
hebrew2 > hebrew1 > english1 > english2 > english3
BUT, it is more convenient to the navigational logic to display it:
hebrew1 > hebrew2 > english1 > english2 > english3
(see sample: http://www.tikshuv.org.il/moodle/mod/forum/view.php?id=5420)

to make it so, i patched the function build_navigation() in lib/weblib.php and changed the following lines...

    //Site name
    if ($site = get_site()) {
        $navlinks[] = array(
                'name' => format_string($site->shortname),
                'link' => "$CFG->wwwroot/",
                'type' => 'home');
    }

to...

    // are we in RTL or LTR course view ? 
    // (make navbar more readable when mixed ltr and rtl nav-words links are displayed)
    // interesting read : http://www.w3.org/TR/i18n-html-tech-bidi/
    if (right_to_left()) {
	  // set the entire content direction  (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right-to-left_mark)
	  $ltrnav = "&#8207";
      } else {
	  // set the entire content direction (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Left-to-right_mark)
	  $ltrnav = "&#8206";
