Result of experiments.
1. Moodle obtains mimetype according to extension of target filename, then cracker can camouflage with mimetype by changing the extension.
2. XSS code (javascript) will run within safari of iPod touch, if mimetype is changed to 'text/html' forcely inside of moodle (ex. any mistakes) even though extension is not 'html'('htm').
3. XSS code will run, if extension is 'html' even though that the mimetype is changed to 'text/plain' forcely inside of moodle.
4. In other words, Safari can display contents of downloaded file as safe text data, if extension is not 'html' and mimetype is 'text/plain'.
5. Built-in viewer of iPod touch can block to display abnormal file which contains XSS code and extension is 'doc' or 'xls' or 'pdf'.

