22/Apr/20 4:25 PM;557058:f8ea19bb-427d-45d0-9c85-c0053eb14e67;I think i found the issue and it isn't my Aurora setup.

 

function query_is_readonly() sets $this->written[$t] on case SQL_QUERY_INSERT and SQL_QUERY_UPDATE which is then checked against $this->slavelatency on case SQL_QUERY_SELECT.

The problem is during a transaction, $this->written isn't being updated so a SELECT  (outside the transaction) immediately after an INSERT or UPDATE inside a transaction will be sent to the slave regardless the slavelatency setting.

 

For example, this query will be sent to the write master:
{noformat}BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED; SAVEPOINT moodle_pg_savepoint

INSERT INTO mdl_page (course,content,contentformat,display,name,intro,introformat,timemodified,displayoptions) VALUES($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8,$9) RETURNING id

RELEASE SAVEPOINT moodle_pg_savepoint; COMMIT{noformat}
Then a select on table mdl_page will be sent to the read slave, ignoring slavelatency.

 

 

I fixed it by adding code to the existing if block for transactions inside the function query_is_readonly(), but I'm not sure if this is the best solution.

 
{code:java}    private function query_is_readonly(int $type, string $sql): bool {
        if ($this->transactions) {
            $now = $this->slavelatency ? microtime(true) : true;
            foreach ($this->table_names($sql) as $t) {
                $this->written[$t] = $now;
            }
            return false;
        }

{code}

