Description:
MDL-20309 changed the update_record function in mdllib.php to add the line "$dataobject->id = (int)$dataobject->id;".  While very much an edge case, if $dataobject->id is not an integer (and non-empty, of course), the id will be set to 0 which could update the incorrect row.  To fix this potential problem, I've changed the preceding "if" statement to read:

    if (! isset($dataobject->id) || $dataobject->id != (string)((int)$dataobject->id) ) {

and removed the "$dataobject->id = (int)$dataobject->id;" line.  By casting to an integer and then back to a string, it ensures that the id is just the integer value with no additional baggage and that the integer is not inadvertently changed during the initial cast.

The enrol/ldap/enrol.php sync_enrollments() function has several efficiency issues.  With no needed enrollment changes, sync_enrollments calls would cause our site to lock for 7 minutes for a cron run.  After the changes described were implemented this run went down to 20 seconds.  Particularly, the sync_enrollments function in HEAD (rev 1.33) calls enrol_ldap_connect(), enrol_ldap_bind(), and fix_course_sortorder() unnecessarily.

If no ldap context is specified for a role, we do not need to connect or bind to LDAP for that role.  At line 130, I added:
    // If no contexts for this role, return immediately
    if (!$CFG->{'enrol_ldap_contexts_role'.$role->id}) {
        return true;
    }

I do not understand why we would call fix_course_sortorder() both before and after each LDAP context (lines 170 and 298).  Shouldn't it only be called if we add a new course?  I replaced the call at 170 with:
    $call_fix_course_sortorder = 0;
After adding a new course at 215 I added:
                 $call_fix_course_sortorder = 1;
And I replaced the call at 298 with:
    if ($call_fix_course_sortorder) {
        fix_course_sortorder();
    }

A common idiom is Moodle is something like

{code}
$action = optional_param('action', '', PARAM_ALPHA);
if (data_submitted() && confirm_sesskey() && $action == 'delete') {
    // ...
}
{code}

I think it would be better to have a new function is_post_with_sesskey() that checks $_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] == 'POST' and sesskey. The the code becomes

{code}
$action = optional_param('action', '', PARAM_ALPHA);
if (is_post_with_sesskey() && $action == 'delete') {
    // ...
}
{code}

which makes it much clearer what is going on.

