Description:
In some circumstances, the function print_error in weblib.php generates a 404 status code response; this occurs in particular if print_error is called in an AJAX context. See code snippet:

    if (! defined('HEADER_PRINTED')) {
        //header not yet printed
        @header('HTTP/1.0 404 Not Found');
        print_header(get_string('error'));
    } else {

However, in this case, no error message is written to Apache's error.log. Since print_error is a very generic function, this makes debugging extremely hard, as no information on the type of error is available. 

I propose to log the error code to the standard error log, like so:

    if (! defined('HEADER_PRINTED')) {
        //header not yet printed
        @header('HTTP/1.0 404 Not Found');
        print_header(get_string('error'));
        error_log ("Moodle error with status 404, error code: {$errorcode}, module: {$module}");
    } else {


Moreover, is 404 the correct status code in these circumstances? Should it not be 500?

-----
Some background on why this occurred to me. Some users on my Moodle installation recently experienced problems with the AJAX course editing features; all AJAX background requests failed. This problem was not reproducible on our test system, so there was no chance of introducing debugging code or similar. (In fact, in the end, it turned out to be a problem with outdated cookies in the user's browser.) This soon became a debugging nightmare. Apache's access.log showed calls to course/rest.php with a status code 404 (as if the file was missing), however there was no message in error.log, even if the "log_errors" PHP option was switched on. There was also no error message on the client side (since these were AJAX requests). It took me quite a while until I realized that these errors were generated in weblib.php; but even then, the reason of the error remained obscure. It could be generated by anything, like an invalid or missing URL parameter, insufficient user rights, or a bad session id (all these conditions eventually produce a call to print_error at some point). In the end, a bad session cookie seemed to be the reason, but strictly speaking, I know only that clearing the browser cookies has helped. The problem as such seems solved now - but better support for debugging would have saved me many hours of work.

