18/Mar/19 6:43 PM;557058:cf548225-e33f-4604-9f4b-33a70b703bc4;David, thanks for the clarification and for the support. If there is some other open issue about this feature please let me know, so that we can continue the discussion there. Otherwise, please let's create one.

The way that you envision this project maybe it is a big one (making AMOS changes, making it completely user-friendly and GUI based). However I think that we can start small and for the first stage we don't need any AMOS changes.

This document describes in details how to customize translations for a local site: [https://docs.moodle.org/36/en/Language_customisation]

At the end it also shows how to backup and restore the customized strings and how to share them with others. This is possible and easy because all the customized strings are saved in *moodledata/lang/xx_local/* (where xx is the code of the language).

One thing that it does not make very clear is that it is possible to import this customized local translation to the main translation in AMOS. Indeed, if we go "AMOS -> Stage", one of the option there is "Import translated strings from file". I am a language admin and maybe this option shows only to language admins, I am not sure, but I believe that it is available to all the translators.

The help for this option says:

{color:#0747a6}If you have your strings translated offline, you can stage them via this form.{color}
 * {color:#0747a6}The file must be valid Moodle PHP strings definition file. Look at */lang/en/* directory of your Moodle installation for examples.{color}
 * {color:#0747a6}Name of the file must match the one with English strings definitons for the given component (like *moodle.php*, *assignment.php* or *enrol_manual.php*).{color}

{color:#0747a6}All strings found in the file will be staged for the selected version and language.{color}

{color:#0747a6}Multiple PHP files can be processed at once if you put them into a ZIP file.{color}

Local customization files can be uploaded here perfectly.

So, the way that I see this project is that it can be just an extension or improvement of the language customization feature, saving any changes in local files. Then these local files can be uploaded/imported to AMOS, to share the changes with the community.

This does not require any changes to AMOS because it already has the basic features to support this workflow. It might be better and more user-friendly to submit the changes directly to AMOS, but it is not strictly required and it can be postponed for a later stage.

Just extending the language customization feature seems to me that is not such a difficult project. Actually I think that even a good student may be able to do it in 2-3 months. Since GSoC is about to start, why not propose it as a project idea and see if any student would like to give it a try?

