28/Apr/10 1:21 AM;557058:c4596910-72fa-4677-8fbf-4d81abaa8214;I've attached a more filled out patch. It uses the external_token table to go back and forth between tokens and user ids. works nicely.  Actually that patch doesnt include all changes but shows how the token processing will work.

One issue that needs to be resolved is the absence of a corresponding row in the table external_service. The table external_token requires externalserviceid. As a temporary work around to see the code run you can just have line 357 of lib/rsslib.php put any number in externalserviceid. As a more permanent fix we can either insert an RSS row in external_service or make external service id optional. Not sure which is preferable.

The other issues are enforcing capabilities and presenting different rss feeds to users in different groups in the forum rss feeds.

Currently the only capability check in rss/file.php is on line 84. It checks 'moodle/course:participate' If the user is in the course they can access any feed in that course. I'm thinking we need a per module callback that does proper capability checking. For example to access a forum's feed the user should have 'mod/forum:viewdiscussion' within that particular forum.

Supporting the partitioning of the forums by group is another requirement not currently supported.

The below algorithm could work:
In /rss/file.php look for %modname%_rss_get_feed() (or whatever)

In mod/%modname%/rsslib.php is %modname%_rss_get_feed():

for the forum module this would look something like this:

//check capabilities
if(has_capability...)

//then
$sql = "select post.id, post.title... where groupid in ($groupids)";
//note group id(s) are within the sql. other params like user id should be in $params[].
//all users in the same set of groups should use the same sql

$filename_in_cache = md5($sql);//hash should be the same for all users with the same group membership

if($modification_date_of_newest_post > file_modification_date($filename_in_cache)) {
//run $sql and export to rss cache
}

//return the file contents from the rss cache


The above should cause there to be one file in the cache per combination of forum groups.

Also, the RSS feeds are updated whenever necessary rather than being reliant on cron.

It may be desirable to add a time delay to prevent the rss feed being updated whenever the forum is altered and an RSS requested. That could cause unnecessary load on busy sites. For example if($newest_page_timestamp>get_file_modification_date($filename_in_cache)+5 minutes) would mean the cache was never refreshed more than every five minutes.

